// #2867 phase 2 split this file into `_tests_/bit/rootpins/{rootpins,objreader}.bit`.
// #2877 found `objreader.bit` byte-identical (modulo an eprint prefix) to
// `_tests_/bit/stwwiring/objreader.bit`, so the Mach-O/ELF relocation reader this
// port had to rebuild (see "WHAT THE PORT HAD TO REBUILD" below) now lives in
// one place, `_tests_/bit/objread/objread.bit`, imported by relative path
// (`import { ... } from "../objread"`) since a directory is its own Bit
// module (§17.1) and this one is not this harness's sibling. This file
// dropped under 800 lines and no longer carries a `bit:lint max-file-lines`
// override.
//
// Runtime-pin cycle gate (#1367) — the Bit port of the deleted harness `rootpins`.
//
// No ported runtime symbol may call the ABI name it is going to become.
//
// Run it: `bit run _tests_/bit/rootpins` from the repository root.
// Configure it by environment, since `bit run` forwards no argv:
//   BIT                 compiler under test    (default bit-out/bin/bit)
//   REPO                repository root        (default .)
//   BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S  per-child deadline     (default 300, clamped to 3600)
//
// THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS TO CATCH, AND WHY NOTHING ELSE CAN SEE IT.
// Almost every ABI function the runtime port had to cover has a Bit PRIMITIVE
// that looks like a ready-made implementation of it:
//
//     export @symbol("bit_rt_host_target") function rtHostTarget(): int {
//       return hostTarget()          // looks like a one-line port
//     }
//
// It is not one. `hostTarget` is not lowered to a hardware instruction — it is
// an `RtFn`, which both backends emit as **a call to `bit_rt_host_target`**
// (`compiler/codegen.bit`'s `rtSymbol`). So the body above compiles to a call
// TO ITSELF: unbounded recursion in every shipped binary, discovered as a stack
// overflow. Every other stage reads green — it builds, it links, it runs (before
// the archive swap the call landed on the still-linked deleted runtime), `--freestanding`
// does not refuse it because `refuseUnpinnedImports` checks pinning and not
// identity, and the differential gates compare check/type/IR dumps while this is
// a property of the emitted object's symbols.
//
// WHY IT READS AN OBJECT INSTEAD OF SCANNING SOURCE. Because the source does not
// contain the answer: `return hostTarget()` names no `bit_rt_*` symbol anywhere,
// the call target is invented by codegen out of an RtFn tag. A grep-based guard
// would have to re-derive the whole primitive -> RtFn -> symbol table and would
// rot the moment one entry moved. The emitted object is the oracle, and it is
// the SAME oracle the linker uses.
//
// WHAT THE RENAME LEFT BEHIND. The Stage-2 port pinned each ABI function under a
// placeholder (`bit_rt_root_host_target`) while the deleted native runtime's
// `root` module still owned
// the real name. #1369 dropped the `_root` infix, so `placeholder` and `real`
// are now the SAME string, `renameOf` is the identity, and the live check is
// plain SELF-REFERENCE. The two constants are kept and applied explicitly rather
// than collapsed away, because collapsing them is a documented trap: setting
// `placeholder = ""` to "finish the job" leaves `real = "bit_rt_"` and
// double-prefixes every check to `bit_rt_bit_rt_host_target`, which matches
// nothing — a vacuous pass.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TWO THINGS THIS PORT FIXES RATHER THAN INHERITS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// 1. THE MODULE LIST IS DISCOVERED, NOT HAND-MAINTAINED, AND COVERAGE IS
//    ASSERTED. The deleted harness carried a literal `modules` array whose stated
//    selection rule ("`bit_rt_root_` is the only prefix the rename touches, so
//    `bit_rt_heap_*` and `bit_rt_port_*` pins are out of scope") went OBSOLETE
//    the moment placeholder == real: post-rename, EVERY `bit_rt_*` definition is
//    at risk of self-reference, because every one of them is a name codegen can
//    emit a call to. A list nobody is forced to update is a list that silently
//    stops covering — the original's own mutation log records `modules` being
//    emptied and the suite staying green.
//
//    So: every directory under `runtime/` holding a `.bit` file that pins a
//    `bit_rt_*` symbol IS a module of this gate, found by walking the tree, and
//    every pin found there must come back as a defined global in THAT module's
//    own object (`coverage A`). Add a pin in a module nobody listed and the gate
//    reddens; move a definition to a new directory and it reddens. Second half
//    (`coverage B`): every `bit_rt_*` name any examined object emits a
//    RELOCATION to must itself be one of the pins — so a symbol the compiler can
//    call that no examined module defines is a hole, reported by name, rather
//    than a silence.
//
// 2. `runtime/alloc` AND `runtime/cryptohw` ARE EXAMINED IN THEIR OWN RIGHT.
//    Their pins landed in other modules' objects only because those objects are
//    not freestanding, so an importer inlines the imported bodies. That is
//    INCIDENTAL coverage: it evaporates the day an importer stops importing, and
//    nothing would say so. Coverage A gives credit only from the object emitted
//    for the directory the pin's source file lives in, so incidental copies
//    cannot pay for a module that was never emitted.
//
// WHY IT PINS A TARGET PER MODULE INSTEAD OF USING THE HOST'S. The property is
// target-independent — an RtFn becomes a call on every backend — but the modules
// are not: a `linux/` provider cannot be emitted for Darwin nor a `darwin/` one
// for Linux (the checker refuses `syscall` and `extern function` respectively,
// even on an uncalled declaration). So the directory name selects the target,
// and both object formats are read back. Fixing the target per module rather
// than following the host is also what makes this gate fail IDENTICALLY
// EVERYWHERE, the pattern #1421 established after a module-state alignment bug
// that was correct on aarch64-macOS and wrong on ELF passed the whole
// host-native golden suite. Nothing is linked or executed, so no `libbitrt.a` is
// needed and there is no skip path: it runs on every host, always.
//
// NOT `--freestanding`, DELIBERATELY. `--freestanding` additionally refuses
// unpinned imports and managed metadata, which several of these modules still
// trip (E0078, #1434/#1421) — and none of that is what this gate is about. An
// ordinary object carries the same relocations. The cost is the incidental
// coverage described above, which is why coverage A attributes by source
// directory instead of trusting whatever a module's object happens to contain.
//
// THE CONDITION IS SELF-REFERENCE, NOT "REFERENCES SOMETHING THIS OBJECT
// DEFINES", and the distinction is the difference between a gate and a nuisance.
// A first cut of the original checked the latter and reported
// `m0$println -> bit_rt_string_concat` alongside the real findings. That is
// correct code: the prelude calling into the runtime is the entire point of a
// runtime. So is one runtime function calling another. Only a definition
// reaching ITSELF is unconditionally a defect.
//
// WHAT THE PORT HAD TO REBUILD. The deleted harness called `bit.buildProject`
// in-process and handed the bytes to `bit.macho_reader`/`bit.elf_reader`.
// Neither is reachable from a Bit program: `compiler/machoreader.bit` and
// `compiler/elfreader.bit` are files of the compiler MODULE, and a `_tests_/bit/`
// harness cannot import them. So this project carries its own object reader,
// now in `../objread/objread.bit` (#2867 phase 2, consolidated with
// `_tests_/bit/stwwiring/`'s byte-identical copy by #2877) — deliberately the
// narrow half of one, since the only question asked of an object is "which
// relocation names which symbol, inside which definition".
//
// It reproduces the deleted seed compiler's `link/object` module's `atomizeModule` rule exactly, because
// the rule IS the gate: an ATOM starts at each defined symbol and spans to the
// NEXT defined symbol in the same section, and a relocation belongs to the atom
// whose span contains its offset. A relocation names a symbol only when that
// symbol is UNDEFINED or GLOBAL (`N_EXT` / non-`STB_LOCAL`), matching
// `SymbolRef.global`; a reference to a local definition is `.local` and is
// skipped, exactly as the deleted harness skipped it.
//
// VACUITY IS THE FAILURE MODE THIS GATE IS MOST EXPOSED TO, and the original
// measured three separate instances of it. Mach-O spells symbols with a leading
// underscore, so for months the entire Darwin half matched zero names and
// asserted NOTHING while reading green; the "did this examine anything" check
// was global, which the working modules satisfied on behalf of the broken one;
// and emptying the module list passed, because a per-module check cannot fire
// for a module never visited. Hence, here: the symbol prefix is derived from the
// target, every module must contribute at least one examined pinned definition,
// BOTH object formats must have been exercised, and the discovered module and
// pin counts carry floors so the examined set cannot be quietly emptied.

import { exists, isDir, readDir, readFile } from "std/fs"
import { envOr, exit } from "std/os"
import { join } from "std/path"
import { hasPrefix, indexOf, parseInt } from "std/strings"
import { now } from "std/time"
import { Obj, ObjSym, atomEnd, badObj, objFail, readElf, readMacho } from "../objread"
import {
  Cfg,
  maxScan,
  tLinux,
  tMacos,
  reportChild,
  runBounded,
  scriptPath,
  timeoutSeconds,
  trimLeadingSpace,
} from "../childrun"

// The placeholder prefix the Stage-2 port pinned its ABI functions under, and
// the real ABI prefix it became. Both are `bit_rt_` since #1369 landed, so
// `renameOf` is the identity — see the header for why they are still two names.
const placeholder: string = "bit_rt_"
const real: string = "bit_rt_"

// Where the runtime tree lives, and how deep the walk may go. `runtime/` is two
// levels (`runtime/net/linux`), so 2 covers it with the recursion still provably
// bounded.
const runtimeDir: string = "runtime"
const maxDepth: int = 2

// The pin syntax scanned for, and the marker that makes a line prose rather than
// code. `runtime/root/floats.bit` documents the exact trap this gate guards by
// QUOTING a pin inside a comment, so a scan that ignored comments would demand a
// definition that deliberately does not exist.
const pinMarker: string = "@symbol(\""
const commentMarker: string = "//"

// Floors on the examined set. Discovery cannot be emptied by editing this file,
// but it CAN be emptied by deleting runtime source, and a gate that examines
// nothing is the failure mode above. Today's tree gives 28 modules and 306 pins;
// the floors sit below that so ordinary work does not trip them, and lowering
// one is a deliberate act that says the runtime shrank.
const minModules: int = 24
const minPins: int = 280

// One runtime module: the directory relative to the repository root, and the
// target its sources can be emitted for.
class Mod { path: string, target: string }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Discovering the modules and their pins
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A `linux/` module needs an ELF target and a `darwin/` one a Mach-O target,
// because the checker refuses `syscall` for Darwin and `extern function` for
// Linux even on an uncalled declaration. A platform-free module builds for
// either; aarch64-macos is chosen so the underscore-spelling half of the reader
// is exercised by the majority of the tree rather than by a lone entry.
fn targetFor(rel: string): string {
  if (hasSuffix(rel, "/linux")) {
    return tLinux
  }
  return tMacos
}

fn hasSuffix(s: string, suf: string): bool {
  if (len(suf) > len(s)) {
    return false
  }
  return s[len(s) - len(suf):len(s)] == suf
}

fn symPrefixFor(target: string): string {
  if (target == tMacos) {
    return "_"
  }
  return ""
}

// `trimLeadingSpace` moved to `../childrun` (#2902): byte-identical to
// `_tests_/bit/stwwiring/`'s copy.

// Every `bit_rt_*` name pinned by a line of `src` that is CODE. One pin per
// line holds throughout this tree; a line whose first non-space characters are
// `//` is prose and contributes nothing.
fn pinsInSrc(src: string, out: []string): []string {
  let pos = 0
  let lines = 0
  while (pos < len(src) && lines < maxScan) {
    lines = lines + 1
    let rel = indexOf(src[pos:len(src)], "\n")
    let line = src[pos:len(src)]
    if (rel >= 0) {
      line = src[pos:pos + rel]
      pos = pos + rel + 1
    } else {
      pos = len(src)
    }
    let body = trimLeadingSpace(line)
    if (hasPrefix(body, commentMarker)) {
      continue
    }
    let at = indexOf(body, pinMarker)
    if (at < 0) {
      continue
    }
    let d = at + len(pinMarker)
    let end = d
    while (end < len(body) && body[end:end + 1] != "\"") {
      end = end + 1
    }
    let name = body[d:end]
    if (hasPrefix(name, placeholder)) {
      out = append(out, name)
    }
  }
  return out
}

// Every `bit_rt_*` pin in the `.bit` files directly inside `abs` — NOT
// recursively, because a subdirectory is a different module (SPEC §17.1) and
// crediting its pins here is exactly the incidental coverage this port refuses.
fn pinsOfDir(abs: string): []string {
  let out = []string(0)
  let names = readDir(abs) catch e {
    eprint("rootpins: cannot list ${abs}: ${e.message()}\n")
    return out
  }
  for name of names {
    if (!hasSuffix(name, ".bit")) {
      continue
    }
    let src = readFile(join(abs, name)) catch e {
      eprint("rootpins: cannot read ${join(abs, name)}: ${e.message()}\n")
      continue
    }
    out = pinsInSrc(src, out)
  }
  return out
}

// Every directory at or under `abs` that pins at least one ABI name, depth-first
// and bounded by `depth`.
fn collectMods(abs: string, rel: string, out: []Mod, depth: int): []Mod {
  if (depth < 0) {
    return out
  }
  if (len(pinsOfDir(abs)) > 0) {
    out = append(out, Mod{ path: rel, target: targetFor(rel) })
  }
  let names = readDir(abs) catch e {
    eprint("rootpins: cannot list ${abs}: ${e.message()}\n")
    return out
  }
  for name of names {
    let sub = join(abs, name)
    if (!isDir(sub)) {
      continue
    }
    out = collectMods(sub, join(rel, name), out, depth - 1)
  }
  return out
}

fn discoverMods(repo: string): []Mod {
  let root = join(repo, runtimeDir)
  if (!isDir(root)) {
    eprint("rootpins: not a directory: ${root} (set REPO)\n")
    return []Mod(0)
  }
  return collectMods(root, runtimeDir, []Mod(0), maxDepth)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pin-cycle check
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `atomEnd` moved to `../objread` (#2902): both harnesses call it only on an
// `Obj` that module already parsed, so it belongs with the object model
// rather than with either caller's own scaffolding.

// `bit_rt_root_host_target` -> `<sp>bit_rt_host_target`, where `sp` is the
// object format's symbol prefix (`_` on Mach-O). `bare` must already have that
// prefix stripped. BUILT, not sliced: `bit_rt_` and `host_target` are not
// contiguous in `bit_rt_root_host_target`, and a slice that looked right on one
// prefix pair would silently mis-derive on another.
fn renameOf(sp: string, bare: string): string {
  return "${sp}${real}${bare[len(placeholder):len(bare)]}"
}

// Scans o.relocs for references to `renamed` landing inside [s.value, end)
// of s.sect -- the self-reference shape checkSelfRef looks for -- printing
// one diagnostic per hit. `ObjReloc.toSym` is the linker's own view of the
// call target — the name codegen actually emitted, not the name the source
// spelled — which is what makes this gate see through the primitive -> RtFn
// -> symbol indirection that no source scan can follow. Returns how many it
// found.
fn countSelfRefRelocs(m: Mod, o: Obj, s: ObjSym, renamed: string, end: int): int {
  let failures = 0
  let j = 0
  while (j < len(o.relocs) && j < maxScan) {
    let r = o.relocs[j]
    j = j + 1
    if (r.toSym != renamed || r.sect != s.sect) {
      continue
    }
    if (r.off < s.value || r.off >= end) {
      continue
    }
    eprint(
        "rootpins: ${m.path}: '${s.name}' references '${r.toSym}'.\n" +
        "  These are ONE symbol, so the call is unbounded self-recursion in every\n" +
        "  binary that links this runtime. The reference is almost certainly a Bit\n" +
        "  primitive that lowers to this RtFn (compiler/lowerprim.bit's primRtName\n" +
        "  and compiler/codegen.bit's rtSymbol): the primitive IS this function, so\n" +
        "  it cannot implement it.\n",
    )
    failures = failures + 1
  }
  return failures
}

// Fails the module on any post-rename self-reference. `counts[0]` accumulates
// the pinned ABI definitions examined, so a module that matched nothing can be
// reported as the vacuity it is rather than passing.
fn checkSelfRef(m: Mod, sp: string, o: Obj, counts: []int): int {
  let failures = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(o.syms) && i < maxScan) {
    let s = o.syms[i]
    i = i + 1
    if (s.sect < 0 || !s.isGlobal || !hasPrefix(s.name, sp)) {
      continue
    }
    let bare = s.name[len(sp):len(s.name)]
    if (!hasPrefix(bare, placeholder)) {
      continue
    }
    counts[0] = counts[0] + 1
    let renamed = renameOf(sp, bare)
    let end = atomEnd(o, s.sect, s.value)
    failures = failures + countSelfRefRelocs(m, o, s, renamed, end)
  }
  return failures
}

// COVERAGE A. Every pin the module's own sources declare must come back as a
// defined global in the module's own object. A pin that does not is either a
// definition that moved to a directory this walk never reached, or a spelling
// the reader no longer matches — both are holes, and both used to be silence.
fn checkPinsDefined(m: Mod, sp: string, o: Obj, pins: []string): int {
  let missing = 0
  for p of pins {
    let want = "${sp}${p}"
    let found = false
    let i = 0
    while (i < len(o.syms) && i < maxScan) {
      if (o.syms[i].sect >= 0 && o.syms[i].isGlobal && o.syms[i].name == want) {
        found = true
      }
      i = i + 1
    }
    if (found) {
      continue
    }
    eprint(
        "rootpins: ${m.path}: pins '${p}' but its object defines no such global " +
        "('${want}'). The gate examined nothing for that name, which is a failure and " +
        "not a pass — the definition moved, the pin is dead, or the symbol prefix for " +
        "${m.target} is wrong.\n",
    )
    missing = missing + 1
  }
  return missing
}

// COVERAGE B. Every `bit_rt_*` name this object emits a relocation to must be a
// pin some examined module declares. A target that is not is a symbol the
// compiler can call and this gate never looked at — precisely the hole a
// hand-maintained module list leaves behind.
fn checkTargetsCovered(m: Mod, sp: string, o: Obj, pinned: map<string, bool>): int {
  let holes = 0
  let seen = map<string, bool>{}
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(o.relocs) && i < maxScan) {
    let t = o.relocs[i].toSym
    i = i + 1
    if (!hasPrefix(t, sp)) {
      continue
    }
    let bare = t[len(sp):len(t)]
    if (!hasPrefix(bare, real) || pinned[bare] || seen[bare]) {
      continue
    }
    seen[bare] = true
    eprint(
        "rootpins: ${m.path}: calls '${bare}', which no examined module pins.\n" +
        "  Either a runtime module defining it is outside ${runtimeDir}/ (so this gate " +
        "never emitted it and cannot say whether it calls itself), or nothing defines it " +
        "at all and the link is about to fail.\n",
    )
    holes = holes + 1
  }
  return holes
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Emitting the objects
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Compiles `<repo>/<path>` for its target as a relocatable object and parses it.
// NOT `--freestanding` — see the header.
fn loadObject(c: Cfg, m: Mod, idx: int): Obj {
  let dir = join(c.repo, m.path)
  if (!isDir(dir)) {
    return objFail("rootpins", "not a module directory: ${dir} (set REPO)")
  }
  let base = "/tmp/bit-rootpins-${now().ns}-${idx}"
  let obj = "${base}.o"
  let cmd = "exec \"${c.bin}\" build \"${dir}\" --emit-obj " + "--target ${m.target} -o \"${obj}\""
  let rc = runBounded("rootpins", c.script, "${base}.log", cmd, c.timeoutMs)
  if (rc != 0) {
    reportChild("rootpins", "${m.path} (${m.target})", rc, "${base}.log")
    fsRemove(obj)
    return badObj()
  }
  fsRemove("${base}.log")
  let raw = readFile(obj) catch e {
    fsRemove(obj)
    return objFail("rootpins", "cannot read emitted object ${obj}: ${e.message()}")
  }
  fsRemove(obj)
  if (m.target == tMacos) {
    return readMacho("rootpins", []u8(raw))
  }
  return readElf("rootpins", []u8(raw))
}

// `runBounded`, `reportChild`, `signalName` and `timeoutSeconds` moved to
// `../childrun` (#2902): byte-identical (modulo an eprint tag) to
// `_tests_/bit/stwwiring/`'s copies.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The union of every module's pins, as a set — the authority coverage B checks
// relocation targets against.
fn pinSet(c: Cfg, mods: []Mod, total: []int): map<string, bool> {
  let set = map<string, bool>{}
  for m of mods {
    for p of pinsOfDir(join(c.repo, m.path)) {
      total[0] = total[0] + 1
      set[p] = true
    }
  }
  return set
}

// Emits and checks every module. `counts` carries [modules examined, Mach-O
// modules, ELF modules] so `main` can prove both readers ran.
fn runMods(c: Cfg, mods: []Mod, pinned: map<string, bool>, counts: []int): int {
  let failed = 0
  let idx = 0
  for m of mods {
    idx = idx + 1
    let sp = symPrefixFor(m.target)
    let o = loadObject(c, m, idx)
    if (!o.ok) {
      eprint(
          "rootpins: could not read the object for ${m.path} (${m.target}); its " +
          "pins are UNCHECKED\n",
      )
      return 0 - 1
    }
    let examined = [0]
    failed = failed + checkSelfRef(m, sp, o, examined)
    failed = failed + checkPinsDefined(m, sp, o, pinsOfDir(join(c.repo, m.path)))
    failed = failed + checkTargetsCovered(m, sp, o, pinned)
    // PER MODULE, not once for the whole run. A global "at least one module
    // contributed" check is satisfied by the working modules while a broken one
    // silently examines nothing — which is exactly how the original's Darwin
    // half read green for months.
    if (examined[0] == 0) {
      eprint(
          "rootpins: ${m.path}: contributed no pinned ABI definitions — the gate " +
          "examined nothing. Either the module stopped pinning ABI names or symbol " +
          "spelling changed and the '${sp}${placeholder}' prefix test no longer matches.\n",
      )
      failed = failed + 1
    }
    counts[0] = counts[0] + 1
    if (m.target == tMacos) {
      counts[1] = counts[1] + 1
    } else {
      counts[2] = counts[2] + 1
    }
  }
  return failed
}

fn main() {
  let c = Cfg{
    bin: envOr("BIT", "bit-out/bin/bit"), repo: envOr("REPO", "."),
    timeoutMs: timeoutSeconds("rootpins") * 1000, script: scriptPath("rootpins"),
  }
  if (!exists(c.bin)) {
    eprint("rootpins: compiler not found: ${c.bin} (set BIT)\n")
    exit(1)
    return
  }
  let mods = discoverMods(c.repo)
  // An empty list makes the loop below a no-op and the whole gate a green no-op
  // with it — measured on the original, which passed with `modules` emptied
  // because a per-module vacuity check cannot fire for a module never visited.
  if (len(mods) < minModules) {
    eprint(
        "rootpins: discovered ${len(mods)} pinning modules under ${runtimeDir}/, floor " +
        "is ${minModules}. The examined set shrank: either the walk stopped finding " +
        "modules (depth, permissions, a moved tree) or runtime source was deleted. " +
        "Lowering the floor is the deliberate way to say the runtime got smaller.\n",
    )
    exit(1)
    return
  }
  let total = [0]
  let pinned = pinSet(c, mods, total)
  if (total[0] < minPins) {
    eprint(
        "rootpins: found ${total[0]} pinned ABI names, floor is ${minPins}. Same " +
        "reasoning as the module floor: a gate that examines less than it used to must " +
        "say so.\n",
    )
    exit(1)
    return
  }
  let counts = [0, 0, 0]
  let failed = runMods(c, mods, pinned, counts)
  // The last child has run, so the shared scratch script is dead: one removal
  // per process, never one per child (`scriptPath` says why).
  fsRemove(c.script)
  if (failed < 0) {
    exit(1)
    return
  }
  // Both readers must have run. The original's whole Darwin half asserted
  // nothing for months while the suite stayed green, so "some modules passed" is
  // not evidence that both object formats were exercised.
  if (counts[1] == 0 || counts[2] == 0) {
    eprint(
        "rootpins: examined ${counts[1]} Mach-O and ${counts[2]} ELF modules — one " +
        "object format was never read, so half this gate asserted nothing\n",
    )
    failed = failed + 1
  }
  if (failed > 0) {
    eprint("rootpins: FAILED — ${failed} finding(s)\n")
    exit(1)
    return
  }
  print(
      "${counts[0]} runtime modules (${counts[1]} Mach-O, ${counts[2]} ELF), " +
      "${total[0]} pinned ABI names: no definition references itself\n",
  )
}
